   172	## Gate results
   173	
   174	### Gate A — PASS
   175	
   176	- A1: `spec.k` pins the function binding, parameter, defining environment, and
   177	  complete translated body. No program-defined callback is hidden: the key is
   178	  the fixed, pure tuple `.index` method. The swapped-body mutation is rejected.
   179	- A2: the result and every active cell are constrained. The proof records both
   180	  list allocations, `heapLoc` changing from `0` to `2`, restoration to module
   181	  environment/scope location, empty call stack, `noRet`, `NoExc`, and exit code
   182	  `0`.
   183	- A3: module scope binds the exact closure, parent scope is the supplied
   184	  builtins scope, argument evaluation goes through the fixed call machinery,
   185	  and the constructed tuple determines the exact bound method supplied to the
   186	  sorter.
   187	- A4: all local equations are true definitions. `numberOrder` and
   188	  `isNumeralCodes` each have one unconditional equation. The recursive token
   189	  predicate has disjoint, total cases and strict structural descent. No local
   190	  equation overlaps inconsistently.
   191	- A5: the prompt example is a realizable witness, and it ran successfully under
   192	  concrete K. The false-result mutation is rejected with exit `1`.
   193	
   194	### Gate B — PASS
   195	
   196	- B1: the precondition exactly requires tokens from the prompt's ten allowed
   197	  words. Empty input and additional ASCII whitespace are admitted; neither
   198	  changes the requested ordering property. Invalid words are explicitly
   199	  excluded.
   200	- B2: valid tokens and output are ASCII, matching the supplied `IntSeq` string
   201	  model. Invalid-token `ValueError`, Unicode whitespace beyond the fixed model,
   202	  and behavior outside the reference subset are excluded and recorded below.
   203	- B3: the K theorem formally characterizes execution in terms of `sortKeyVS`.
   204	  Its interpretation as stable ascending keyed sort is conditionally trusted
   205	  from the supplied semantics and empirically supported, not claimed as a
   206	  theorem derived in this proof.
   207	- B4: the tuple ranks the ten words from zero to nine, so tuple `.index`
   208	  supplies their numeric order. The implementation matches the prompt example,
   209	  boundary cases, duplicates, and broad differential samples.
   210	
   211	### Gate C — PASS
   212	
   213	- The trust ledger below names every component outside the theorem and its
   214	  dependents.
   215	- `differential_test.py`, `generate_k_tests.py`,
   216	  `generated-concrete-tests.py`, `generated-concrete-tests.mpy`, mutation specs,
